\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The decomposition applies to cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas exhibit centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). Separability is confirmed on FewRel and DocRED (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}); logit-scale evidence is inconclusive. The RE effect replicates across four models from three families (8B--70B; Gemini Flash), two formats, five prompts, and two RE datasets; the NER null is confirmed on two models.

\paragraph{Embedding similarity and format independence.}
SCS captures semantic proximity in LLMs' type-resolution space, amplifying from pairwise $R^2 = 1.3\%$ to batch-level 22--58\% (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}). BCS predicts RE but not NER; format independence is confirmed (Appendix~\ref{app:format-independence}). RE errors concentrate in type substitutions \citep{tam-2024-let-me-speak}; NER errors scatter across miss, boundary, and type modes \citep{schall-2025-hidden-cost}. SCS likely proxies conceptual overlap beyond label similarity (Appendix~\ref{app:renaming}).\footnote{Partial correlation controlling for degree centrality: FewRel $r_{\text{partial}} = -0.537$, VIF${}\,=\,1.18$; DocRED $r_{\text{partial}} = -0.348$, $p < 0.001$, though near-collinear with centrality (VIF${}\,=\,50.9$). See Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_partial_corr}.}

\paragraph{Practical implications and conclusion.}
\label{sec:conclusion}
For NER, reducing candidate types is the primary lever; for RE, flagging pairs with SCS${}\,>\,0.85$ and monitoring BCS (typical gap 2--4\,pp; extreme 25--31\,pp). In sum, training-free LLM extraction degrades along two separable dimensions---count-associated recall decline and confusability-associated type substitution---replicated across four models (8B--70B; Gemini Flash) for RE, with NER null confirmed on two models; schema decomposition fails via merge amplification and routing failure.
